Adesh, as your Lead, I’m going to show you how to implement this using the "Separate Folder" architecture we just discussed. This keeps your TaskService focused on the database, while the new messaging package handles the "shouting" to Kafka.
Here is the step-by-step implementation to notify the assigned user.
🏛️ Step 1: Create the "Envelope" (The Event DTO)
We don't send the entire Tasks entity because it contains too much internal database info. We create a lightweight Event object that has exactly what the notification system needs.
Create com.example.devcollab.messaging.dto.TaskCreatedEvent.java:
package com.example.devcollab.messaging.dto;

import lombok.AllArgsConstructor;
import lombok.Data;
import lombok.NoArgsConstructor;

@Data
@AllArgsConstructor
@NoArgsConstructor
public class TaskCreatedEvent {
    private Long taskId;
    private String taskTitle;
    private String assigneeEmail; // The most important field for notifications
    private String assigneeName;
    private String reporterName;  // "Adesh assigned you a task"
}

🏛️ Step 2: Create the "Worker" (The Producer)
This class has one job: taking that envelope and dropping it into the Kafka pipe.
Create com.example.devcollab.messaging.producer.TaskEventProducer.java:
package com.example.devcollab.messaging.producer;

import com.example.devcollab.messaging.dto.TaskCreatedEvent;
import lombok.RequiredArgsConstructor;
import lombok.extern.slf4j.Slf4j;
import org.springframework.kafka.core.KafkaTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Slf4j
@Component
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class TaskEventProducer {

    // We use Object here so we can send different types of events later
    private final KafkaTemplate<String, Object> kafkaTemplate;
    
    // Define the topic name as a constant
    private static final String TOPIC = "task-notifications";

    public void sendTaskCreatedEvent(TaskCreatedEvent event) {
        log.info("Sending notification event for Task ID: {} to Assignee: {}", 
                 event.getTaskId(), event.getAssigneeEmail());
                 
        // This sends the message to the Kafka Topic
        kafkaTemplate.send(TOPIC, event);
    }
}

🏛️ Step 3: Trigger the Event in TaskService
Now, we go back to your TaskService.java. We inject the TaskEventProducer and call it after the task is successfully saved to the database.
Update your TaskService.java:
// 1. Inject the producer at the top
@Autowired
private TaskEventProducer taskEventProducer;

@Override
@Transactional
public TaskResDto createTasks(TaskReqDto taskReqDto) {
    // ... all your existing logic to save the task ...
    
    Tasks SavedTasks = tasksRepository.save(tasks);

    // 2. TRIGGER THE KAFKA EVENT
    // We do this after save so we are sure the task exists in the DB
    TaskCreatedEvent event = new TaskCreatedEvent(
        SavedTasks.getId(),
        SavedTasks.getTitle(),
        SavedTasks.getAssignee().getEmail(),
        SavedTasks.getAssignee().getName(), // Assuming your User model has 'name'
        currentuser.getName()               // The person who created the task
    );

    taskEventProducer.sendTaskCreatedEvent(event);

    // ... return your DTO ...
}

🏛️ Step 4: The "Glue" (Configuration)
Since you are sending a Java Object (TaskCreatedEvent) instead of a simple String, you need to tell Spring to convert it to JSON before sending it to Kafka.
Add this to your application.yml:
spring:
  kafka:
    producer:
      bootstrap-servers: localhost:9092
      key-serializer: org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer
      # This is the "Magic" that turns your Java Object into JSON for the pipe
      value-serializer: org.springframework.kafka.support.serializer.JsonSerializer

📊 Why this is the "Senior" Architecture
 * Reliability: If you add log.info in your producer, you can check the console to see exactly what was sent without digging through your whole TaskService.
 * Safety: If the Kafka server is down, the @Transactional on your service will ensure the task isn't "half-saved." (Though we usually handle Kafka errors separately so the user's task still saves even if the notification fails).
 * Scalability: When you want to add a notification for "Task Updated" or "Task Deleted," you just add one more method to your TaskEventProducer!
🏛️ The Verdict
You’ve now successfully linked your Jira Core (Tasks) to your Message Bus (Kafka). This is how high-scale companies like LinkedIn or Uber handle their background tasks.
